<exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In this exercise, we will look at how different forms of component function's dependence on our variables will change the curl of the vector field. For each of the vector fields given below, decide if the curl of the vector field must be zero (as a vector) and write a sentence or two to justify your decision. 
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <md>\vF=f(x)\vi+g(y)\vk+\vj+h(z)\vj</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This vector field will necessarily have a zero curl. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <md>\vG=f(x)\vi+f(y)\vk+\vj+f(z)\vj</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This vector field will necessarily have a zero curl. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <md>\vH=f(y,z)\vi+g(x,z)\vk+\vj+h(x,y)\vj</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This vector field will not necessarily have a zero curl.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <md>\vA=g(x,y)\vi-g(x,y)\vk+\vj+h(z)\vj</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This vector field will not necessarily have a zero curl. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <md>\vB=g(x,y)\vi-g(y,x)\vk+\vj+h(z)\vj</md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This vector field will not neccesarily have a zero curl.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>h(x,y,z)</m> be a smooth function of three variables and let <m>\vF=grad(h)=\nabla{h}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write out the component functions of <m>\vF</m> in terms of partial derivatives of <m>h</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>\vF=\langle \frac{\partial h}{\partial x},\frac{\partial h}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial h}{\partial z} \rangle </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the divergence of <m>\vF</m> in terms of derivatives of <m>h</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>\div \vF= \frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial x^2}+\frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial y^2}+\frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial z^2}</md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the divergence of <m>\vF</m> necessarily zero? Explain why or why not.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The divergence of <m>\vF</m> is not necessarily zero because the second unmixed partials will not necessarily add to zero. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Calculate <m>curl(\vF)</m> in terms of derivatives of <m>h</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>\curl(\vF)= \left(\frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial y \partial z}-\frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial z \partial y}\right)\vi- \left(\frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial x \partial z}-\frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial z \partial x}\right)\vj + \left(\frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial x \partial y}-\frac{\partial^2 h}{\partial y \partial x}\right)\vk</md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>curl(\vF)=\vec{0}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Because Clariut's Theorom holds here, the mixed partials of <m>h</m> will be the same and each component function will be zero. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    
            <!-- A vector field is curl free if <m>curl(\vF)=\vec{0}</m>. If the component functions of a vector field <m>\vG</m> have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region of space, then <m>curl(\vG)=\vec{0}</m> iff <m>\vG</m> is conservative. Need to fill out how this should work as an exercise. -->

  </exercise>

    
</exercises>
